{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '85,245p' test/yang/clojure_collection_order_test.cljc; sed -n '110,150p' src/cljc/dao/host/bytes.cljc; sed -n '378,418p' docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md\"","aggregated_output":"\n(defn- element-keys\n  \"The RULING 1 sort keys of a set constructor's elements (one root each),\n   in emitted order.\"\n  [node]\n  (mapv vector (rest (child-roots node))))\n\n\n(defn- strictly-ascending?\n  \"True when `ks` sorts strictly ascending by jing/canonical-compare: every\n   adjacent pair compares negative. The strict form deliberately does NOT\n   certify a tie (equal adjacent keys are equal executable content and a\n   legal emitted order, but not a strictly ascending one) -- blocks that\n   construct a tie assert it directly or compare against the sequence's\n   own non-descending sort instead.\"\n  [ks]\n  (every? (fn [[a b]] (neg? (jing/canonical-compare a b)))\n          (partition 2 1 ks)))\n\n\n(defn- key-literals\n  \"The literal values at the key positions of a map constructor (every\n   second operand after the seed), for reading an emitted order.\"\n  [node]\n  (mapv (fn [o] (when (= :literal (:type o)) (:value o)))\n        (take-nth 2 (rest (:operands node)))))\n\n\n(defn- project-batch\n  \"One compiled ast through the production encoder envelope under `token`.\"\n  ([ast] (project-batch ast \"order-a\"))\n  ([ast token]\n   (let [p (encoder/project (encoder/source-envelope :cc4/replay token [ast]))]\n     {:tree (first (:yin/batch p))\n      :rows (:yin/frontend-metadata p)})))\n\n\n(defn- digest-snapshot\n  \"The equality snapshot of one projected batch: root, the canonical-sorted\n   C1 marker relation, the sorted occurrence-key vector, and all three\n   instruction formats' call-provenance -- the cohort's model, with nothing\n   normalized away.\"\n  [{:keys [tree rows]}]\n  (let [c1 (vec (sort jing/canonical-compare\n                      (filter #(= :yang/call-site (nth % 3)) rows)))\n        keys (vec (sort jing/canonical-compare\n                        (into [] (comp (filter #(= :yang/call-site (nth % 3)))\n                                       (map #(vec (take 3 %)))) rows)))\n        sem (linearize/project-rows tree nil rows)\n        stack (derive/stack-image (:vector sem)\n                                  {:segment-address :order/stack\n                                   :call-provenance (:call-provenance sem)})\n        register (derive/register-image (:vector sem)\n                                        {:segment-address :order/register\n                                         :call-provenance (:call-provenance sem)})]\n    {:root (:root tree)\n     :applications (count keys)\n     :keys-digest (jing/content-hash keys)\n     :c1-digest (jing/content-hash c1)\n     :instruction-provenance-digest\n     (jing/content-hash [(:call-provenance sem)\n                         (:call-provenance stack)\n                         (:call-provenance register)])}))\n\n\n(defn- compile-snapshot\n  \"Compile `form`, project it, digest it.\"\n  [form]\n  (digest-snapshot (project-batch (yang/compile form))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Execution routes (RULING 2/3: walker, semantic, stack, register)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def ^:private registry\n  (-> (module/empty-registry)\n      module/register-cell-module\n      data/register-data-module))\n\n\n(def ^:private base-opts\n  {:make-stream tu/make-stream\n   :capability-secret tu/secret\n   :primitives vm/primitives\n   :modules registry})\n\n\n(def ^:private load-semantic-ast\n  (vm/fresh-code-loader semantic/load-ast vm/ast-contract))\n\n\n(def ^:private lanes\n  \"The four execution routes the behavior tests run.\"\n  [:ast-walker :semantic :stack :register])\n\n\n(defn- run-lane\n  \"Run compiled `ast` on one execution route.\"\n  [lane ast env]\n  ;; the walker/semantic machines take :env; the debruijn machines take\n  ;; :free-env for free names -- both spell the same test-supplied bindings\n  (let [opts (assoc base-opts :env env :free-env env)]\n    (case lane\n      :ast-walker (vm/eval (tu/create-vm opts) ast)\n      :semantic (vm/run (load-semantic-ast (semantic/create-vm opts)\n                                           (vm/ast->datoms ast)))\n      :stack (vm/run (dvm/create-vm\n                       (:image (derive/stack-image\n                                 (:vector (linearize/project ast))))\n                       (assoc opts :contract vm/stack-contract)))\n      :register (vm/run (rvm/create-vm\n                          (derive/register-image\n                            (:vector (linearize/project ast)))\n                          (assoc opts :contract vm/register-contract))))))\n\n\n(defn- run-on\n  \"Run compiled `ast` on every execution route with `env`, returning\n   lane -> value.\"\n  ([ast] (run-on ast {}))\n  ([ast env]\n   (into {} (map (fn [lane] [lane (vm/value (run-lane lane ast env))]))\n         lanes)))\n\n\n(defn- run-form\n  \"Compile and run `form` on every route with `env`.\"\n  ([form] (run-form form {}))\n  ([form env] (run-on (yang/compile form) env)))\n\n\n(defn- trace-of\n  \"The observable evaluation of `form` on every route: the lane's value\n   (or its raised ex-data) together with the ordered log the env-bound\n   `tap` host function recorded during THAT lane's run. `tap` echoes its\n   argument, so a log is exactly the order that lane consumed the operands\n   in; a lane whose operand raises still reports the events that happened\n   before the raise.\"\n  [form env]\n  (let [ast (yang/compile form)\n        log (atom [])\n        env (assoc env (quote tap) (fn [x] (swap! log conj x) x))]\n    (into {} (map (fn [lane]\n                    (reset! log [])\n                    (let [result (try\n                                   {:value (vm/value (run-lane lane ast env))}\n                                   (catch #?(:clj Exception, :cljs :default, :cljd Object) e\n                                     {:raised (ex-data e)}))]\n                      [lane (assoc result :log @log)])))\n          lanes)))\n\n\n(defn- permutations-of\n  \"Every ordering of the items, the plain recursive enumeration.\"\n  [items]\n  (if (empty? items)\n    [[]]\n    (mapcat (fn [i]\n              (map (fn [rest-perm] (into [i] rest-perm))\n                   (permutations-of (vec (remove #{i} items)))))\n    (check-index! 'slice start 0 n)\n    (check-index! 'slice end start n))\n  #?(:cljd (Uint8List.fromList (.sublist ^Uint8List bs start end))\n     :clj (java.util.Arrays/copyOfRange ^bytes bs (int start) (int end))\n     :cljs (.slice bs start end)))\n\n\n(defn equal?\n  \"P. True when byte arrays `a` and `b` hold the same bytes.\"\n  [a b]\n  (let [n (length a)]\n    (and (= n (length b))\n         (loop [i 0]\n           (or (= i n)\n               (and (= (byte-at a i) (byte-at b i))\n                    (recur (inc i))))))))\n\n\n(defn from-vector\n  \"P, fresh. A new host byte array of `xs`, a vector of native integers\n   0 to 255; any other element is refused `:out-of-range` before\n   anything is built.\"\n  [xs]\n  (when-not (vector? xs)\n    (refuse 'from-vector :unsupported-value \"not a vector\"))\n  (run! #(check-byte! 'from-vector %) xs)\n  #?(:cljd (Uint8List.fromList xs)\n     :clj (byte-array (map unchecked-byte xs))\n     :cljs (js/Uint8Array.from (into-array xs))))\n\n\n(defn to-vector\n  \"P. The bytes of `bs` as a vector of native integers 0 to 255.\"\n  [bs]\n  (let [n (length bs)]\n    (loop [i 0\n           out (transient [])]\n      (if (< i n)\n        (recur (inc i) (conj! out (byte-at bs i)))\n        (persistent! out)))))\n\nexample decimal `1.0` (exponent `-1`, mantissa `10`, a one-byte shortest\nhead) over decimal `1.00` (exponent `-2`, mantissa `100`, a two-byte\nshortest head); (3) if the two encodings are the same length too, return\nwhichever sorts first by canonical CBOR bytes, the same unsigned bytewise\norder this profile already uses for map keys and set elements, so no new\nordering machinery is needed for the final, rarely-reached tiebreak. All\nthree cases are independent of argument order and of which host runs them:\n`(min 1 1.0)` and `(min 1.0 1)` both return `1`.\n\n- Provide a portable `float64` constructor/carrier. JavaScript callers use\n  it for integral floating-point values such as `1.0`; ordinary integral\n  JavaScript numbers remain integers, except native negative zero, which\n  is floating-point content so its sign survives. Decoding floating-point content on\n  JavaScript returns the carrier so re-encoding cannot lose its kind.\n  Carrying float kind is the producer's obligation; the codec never infers\n  it. The Python frontend's ruling on meeting it is `yang.antlr.md`\n  section 8.5.5.\n- The JavaScript float64 carrier refuses numeric and default coercion: its\n  `valueOf` throws the refusal `:carrier-coercion` (raised through the\n  codec's refusal helper, so `refusal` reads it; it is not a corpus\n  class and not a lift outcome). `carrier + 1`, `carrier - 1`,\n  `carrier < 3`, `Number(carrier)` and `\"\" + carrier` therefore throw\n  instead of answering a string or a bare Number that lost the float's\n  kind. String conversion is unchanged, since it asks `toString` first:\n  `str`, `String(carrier)`, `.toString()` and `pr-str` print as before.\n  So are equality, hashing, map and set keys, canonical bytes, NaN\n  normalization and signed zero, which read the payload directly; no\n  fixture changes. Only Float64 is hardened; the Decimal and Rational\n  carriers and the query arithmetic guards below are unchanged. Generic\n  arithmetic over a decoded carrier is still not portable: the JVM and\n  Dart compute on their native double, JavaScript refuses. A profile that\n  computes on floats portably reads the payload through an explicit\n  accessor (Python's `data/float-value`, `yang.antlr.md` 8.5.5).\n- Encode floating-point content on every host as tag 27,\n  `dao.jing/float64`, with eight big-endian IEEE-754 bytes. This avoids\n  Boring's native-number differences between JVM and JavaScript, verified\n  against its compatibility policy above: integral JS numbers encode as\n  integers while JVM `1.0` remains a float under `:canonical`.\n  Widen native float32 values to float64, preserve signed zero and\n  infinities, and canonicalize NaNs to quiet NaN bits `0x7ff8000000000000`.\n  **The Jing profile emits no native CBOR floating-point whatsoever**;\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
