{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'RULING 3|effect|once|corpus|failure|transfer|fixture' ../datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1795017757000-architect-cc4-dart-map-order-ruling.gpt-6-astra.findings.md | head -75; cat ../datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1795109958000-reviewer-fixorder-r3.prompt.md; ls -lt target | head -32\"","aggregated_output":"6:Scope of this session: read-only investigation, with this findings file as the sole authored artifact. No compiler, tests, fixtures, manifests, or cc4-worktree files were changed. No runtime tests were run.\n31:Compute each child AST and its sort key once per emitter invocation; sorting must not recompile or evaluate it. Keep the original AST, including its attached frontend/source metadata, with its paired record. The projected rows used to obtain a sort key are temporary compiler data, not published roots, origins, expansion events, or a new persisted ordering table. No new canonical field is needed.\n37:Sort keys name the compiled children **at this emission boundary**. A later macro expansion or global-name rewrite may change their addresses; it must preserve the already-selected operand sequence, not re-sort an existing application by its new child addresses. Ordering assertions inspect the emitter boundary; whole-pipeline assertions inspect replay equality and execution. This distinction prevents normalization from becoming a second, effect-reordering optimization pass.\n52:**MUST preserve:** the VM's application evaluation rule, operator lookup, seeds, key/value association, multiplicity, tail behavior, and normal exception/continuation behavior. On a successful construction every key and value is evaluated once; within a map pair the key precedes its value. Sets evaluate each retained element expression once even when evaluated values coincide. The constructor is invoked only after its operands finish, exactly as today. An exception or transfer of control during an operand prevents the later evaluations that the existing VM would not reach. Sorting is compile-time inspection of code, never evaluation of keys or values.\n54:**The order between pairs/elements is henceforth the canonical order in RULING 1.** It is executable order: the VM consumes that operand vector left to right. The frontend MUST NOT pretend this is an observationally invisible serialization change for every possible program. Effectful operands, competing failures, continuation capture, and distinct key expressions evaluating to an equal key can observe the selected order. For equal evaluated keys, ordinary `assoc`'s later-pair replacement behavior remains; which pair is later is now determined canonically. A shadowed constructor still receives the canonically ordered operands through the existing lookup rules.\n56:There is an unavoidable boundary to “runtime evaluation semantics unchanged”: VM transition semantics and explicitly ordered syntax remain unchanged; an accidental old host traversal is not preserved as a portable language order. No one permutation can preserve two different old hosts' observable traces while also producing identical operand bytes. If “unchanged” means preserving each old host's trace for every effectful unordered form, that stronger requirement is incompatible with universal canonical reordering and is **not** a claim of this ruling. This ruling explicitly fixes the frontend's previously host-dependent choice and requires a frontend revision change. The arity-error map's unchanged built-in computation and distinct constant keys make that particular repair value-preserving.\n58:Programs requiring a particular sequence must express it with existing ordered forms, for example `let` bindings that compute values in order followed by collection construction, or an explicit ordered constructor application. This round introduces no implicit purity inference, effect suppression, or new ordered-map metadata protocol.\n64:## RULING 3 — new fix-round bounds and acceptance contract\n84:2. **Map representation/permutation property.** For a recorded corpus of distinct source-entry pairs, construct extensionally identical maps using insertion permutations and available ordinary array-map/hash-map representations. For sizes 0–4 enumerate all permutations; include sizes 8, 9, 16, and 32 with recorded forward, reverse, rotated, and fixed shuffled permutations so representation thresholds are crossed. Include constant keys/dynamic values, dynamic keys, heterogeneous scalar and quoted collection keys, nested maps/sets/vectors, floats, generated destructuring names, duplicate subtrees, and reader/non-reader metadata. At least one dynamic expression must reach the emitter in each nonconstant case. For fixed options and admission/expansion inputs, every permutation/representation on every host MUST yield identical canonical tree bytes/root, full C1/key vectors, and per-format provenance. Compare byte content, not host array identity or printed forms. Constant cases separately assert the literal fast path.\n87:5. **Executable behavior.** Run portable fixtures through walker, semantic, stack, and register execution where those existing routes support them, on the four host lanes. Record an observable trace using the existing test harness's explicit effects/streams: key-before-value, canonical pair/element order, no duplication, and no later event after an operand failure/continuation transfer. Include dynamic keys colliding at runtime and assert the canonically last value wins; include set-value collisions. Compare pure distinct-key results with the unchanged mathematical map/set contents. Explicit `assoc`/`conj` calls, dynamic vectors, `let`, and `do` MUST retain written order; swapping their effectful arguments MUST remain observable and their ordered canonical rows MUST remain distinct. These controls prevent an invalid global sorter from passing.\n88:6. **Entry points and generation.** Cover `compile-form`, `compile`, both relevant `compile-program` modes, and `lower-unit`. Keep the real production macro/twin-call fixture and the complete expansion-record chain in the equality gate. Prove that pre-expansion collection normalization does not fabricate or erase expansion origins. Include compiler-generated arity maps rather than testing only hand-built user maps.\n106:Keep the r2 divergence file, raw logs, and findings immutable as historical defect evidence. Archive the original active snapshots/captures under an explicitly pre-fix name before replacing any active fixture; publish a before/after ledger keyed by compiler revision and input pins. Re-pinning a failing host in isolation is not a repair. A JVM address changing too is expected and is not grounds to preserve the old broken ordering.\n114:**MUST:** advance the Clojure frontend manifest revision from the inspected `\"c1-1\"` to `\"c1-2\"` and declare its lowering profile as `:yang.clojure/constructed-collections-v1` (if the dispatch base has already advanced, choose and record a fresh nonconflicting successor before recording expectations). Keep the C1 marker value `1` and the existing C1 support claim: identity/coverage schema has not changed. Keep SPI and UAST/execution grammar versions unchanged. The shared UAST-to-instruction lowerer has not changed for a given UAST, so its own lowering-profile stamp must not be bumped merely because this frontend now supplies different input trees. Pin the compiler commit and this frontend ordering profile with the replay fixtures.\nCreated-GMT: 2026-11-19 17:39:18 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-20 00:39:18 +0700\nCoding-Agent: codex\nSession-ID: pending (provider-generated)\n\n# Task: Review — fix r3 delta against the sign-off blockers B2/B3/B4\n\nRole: Reviewer (compiler provenance). Author: glm-5.3 (GLM family — you are independent). Review ONLY the r3 delta; r1/r2 findings and the prior review chain stand.\n\n## What changed since the sign-off review (REMAINING-BLOCKERS, 1795101450000)\n\nWorktree /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-fixorder (uncommitted): exactly two authorized files — test/yang/clojure_collection_order_test.cljc (+436/−27) and test/resources/yang/clojure/constructed-collections-legacy-c1-1.cbor (11-byte bogus string → 5,403-byte true artifact, sha 9673c614…). Emitter/manifest/cohort/J2 untouched (verify). Evidence: collab/yang-clojure/1795104744000-fixorder-r3-evidence/. Landing evidence mirrored: 1795110000000-jupiter-g2-gate-evidence/.\n\n## Verify against the architect's dispositions\n\n- **B2**: fixture bytes == decoded embedded copy asserted portably on every host (nio/fs/dart-io, :cljd-gated require); c1-1 root + old {0 :b} execution validated; historical reports unedited.\n- **B3**: seed reduced mod 2^31−2 before first product (verify the reduction makes JVM/Node IDENTICAL shuffles — recompute the size-8 vector yourself; it must match the architect's JVM column [3 0 1 2 5 7 6 4]); recorded generator pins sizes 8/9/16/32; 18-case corpus-reference bundle compared per-host (cross-host equality, not within-host only); permutation checks retained. Assess the reported float-carrier dependency handling (bare integral double divergence — reported, archived, corpus moved to non-integral floats) — is that honest and in-bounds?\n- **B4**: discriminating metadata control ± {:sem true} + empty-metadata control; derived canonical-prefix failure assertions on all four routes with exact ex-data; continuation-transfer fixture with raw AST capture/invoke (pre-transfer operands exactly once, post-transfer never); set effect-order tap trace with colliding occurrences.\n- **B1**: recorded open (J2 owner), not simulated — confirm.\n- Gates: JVM 61/92,082 green (orchestrator re-ran); Node/Dart lanes in the r3 evidence — check counts vs claims.\n\nBounds: READ-ONLY except your findings file in the worktree collab/yang-clojure/ (name suffixed .gpt-6.2-sol). Verdict: APPROVE or REMAINING-FINDINGS with dispositions. If the delt